760":94,"5761":165,"5762":128,"5763":127,"5764":168,"5765":77,"5766":43,"5767":32,"5768":103,"5769":323,"5770":231,"5771":319,"5772":306,"5773":33,"5774":3750,"5775":4732,"5776":1364,"5777":1330,"5778":1345},"orderedDocs":[[124,"—"],[148,"—"],[151,"—"],[1392,"—"],[4650,"\"Yes\" means supported. \"Conditional\" means it depends on configuration or on the hawkBit server; see the notes after the table."],[2889,"(always)"],[2042,"**`ACTIVE`**: the record is enabled. Also check `Not Before` and `Not After`: this state does not establish time validity."],[4720,"**`artifact_encryption`** is reported unsupported only when none of `swugenerator_path`, `symkms_endpoint` and `kms_endpoint` is configured. Each build option still needs its own settings: see Signing and encryption."],[522,"**`default_action` with `base_paths`** decides what happens when a request falls under a path prefix the schema owns but no explicit route matches. `deny` — the value the Job Manager schema ships with — turns the implicit denial of unmapped paths into an explicit, auditable statement; `allow` opens unmapped endpoints under that prefix to any identity that matches an active principal."],[2043,"**`EXPIRED`**: it has passed its expiration date. The monitor detects this periodically; it is not a state that should be assigned manually."],[5753,"**`helm upgrade --wait` times out and `helm status` reports the release as `failed`.** With local KMS or VA storage this is expected on the first run: the new pods cannot become ready before their data is copied. The hooks ran and the manifests and PVCs were applied, so complete step 4 and re-run the same `helm upgrade` command. If other pods are also not ready, check their logs, because something else is wrong."],[5755,"**`helm upgrade` fails with an unknown-key validation error.** Remove or rename the legacy values — typically `toolbox`, `auth.oidc.apiGateway` or `auth.authorization` — because the strict schema rejects keys it does not know."],[2045,"**`INACTIVE`**: it is disabled without representing a definitive revocation."],[5229,"**`non-swu` packages** (`.../package`, and `hawkbit` `raw` modules) are always encrypted with **AES-GCM**, chosen by key length (16, 24 or 32 bytes). The requested name is only recorded, and a 20-byte Ascon-80pq key is refused. The result is `nonce || ciphertext`."],[4723,"**`prebuilt_deliverable`**: a hawkBit pack is never built directly. A `swu-prebuilt` catalog module accepts a finished `.swu` instead."],[2044,"**`REVOKED`**: it was invalidated before expiring, with a reason and a timestamp."],[521,"**`skip_authz: true` on a route** allows any identity that matches an active principal to reach it, even one with no granted policies. Use it for paths such as `/openapi.json` or `/health` that every authenticated caller should be able to access. The request still has to authenticate successfully: a credential that fails validation or matches no active principal is rejected before this setting is considered. A `skip_authz` route cannot declare constraints, and it wins over any other schema's route or default action for the same request."],[4721,"**`software_module_composition`, `standalone_software_modules` and `software_module_replace`** depend on the hawkBit server having the pre-seeded `os` and `application` module types and the `os_app` or `app` set type. The service probes this with a 5 second timeout, caches the result for about five minutes and assumes support when hawkBit is unreachable. When the probe fails, all three flags are reported unsupported together."],[4722,"**`software_module_deliverables`** is always reported supported in hawkBit mode. Building a `swu-build` module still needs a `swugenerator_path`, and without one the build answers `501`."],[1497,"**`UPDATE_CERTIFICATE`**: written when a certificate's preventive renewal window activates — that is, when the `triggered` field of the preventive renewal delta flips from `false` to `true`. This action tells the device it must start the re-enrollment process to renew its certificate before it expires."],[1498,"**`UPDATE_TRUST_ANCHOR_LIST`**: written to all devices of a DMS when the DMS's `ManagedCAs` list changes. This action tells the device it must update its trusted CA store."],[3760,"**A new `native` pack version inherits the previous version's encryption settings for `.swu` builds.** `POST .../new` copies them, and a `.swu` build with no encryption fields does not clear them. After a per-device build, every later version of that pack still reports `per-device`, keeps the old `uncovered_devices`, and device downloads fail with `404` (`SWU not available for device ...`). Use a separate pack for each encryption variant. (`.../package` builds reset these fields.)"],[3338,"**A tripped error threshold.** hawkBit then pauses the rollout, and the service reports the launch as `cancelled`. `resume` and `rollout` both answer `400` for it."],[797,"**Active**"],[5720,"**Add `services.authz`.** Configure the JWKS endpoint, the database mappings and the bootstrap principal:"],[1727,"**Administration**"],[2277,"**Algorithm, Key Size & Strength**: algorithm, parameters and strength level."],[2292,"**Algorithm**: must match the algorithm used for the signature."],[2285,"**Algorithm**: signing algorithm available for the key type."],[2242,"**Aliases**: alternative names associated with the key."],[2275,"**Aliases**: alternative names."],[3141,"**Allow previous-version download**"],[5767,"**AMQP.** The backend connects to the broker with the expected TLS settings, and the cleanup policy for SSE queues is in place."],[5773,"**API clients.** Scripts and consoles no longer use the old DMS layout (flat `additional_validation_cas`, or the legacy `mtls`, `apikey` and `oidc` fields), send DMS updates with the ID in the URL, and match on the new certificate `type` values."],[681,"**API-key headers are restricted.** A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or receiving services trust — for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded` — and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-`. Comparisons are case-insensitive."],[3545,"**Approval and error thresholds.** With `auto: true`, the next batch is released once at least `approval_threshold` percent of the current batch has succeeded (0 means the whole batch). In native mode the campaign is cancelled when `error_threshold` percent of all its devices have failed; in hawkBit mode it stops when more than that percentage of a batch fails. A failed canary pauses a native campaign instead: resume it to retry the canary."],[3762,"**Artifact names are global.** A name can only be reused with a higher `version`, and the API answers a violation with `500`, not a `4xx`."],[3153,"**Artifacts to include**"],[539,"**Atomic actions** are evaluated on a concrete instance. For certificates these include `read`, `metadata-update`, `status-update` and `delete`; for a CA there are also `sign`, `reissue` and other lifecycle operations."],[604,"**Audit**: reviews configuration and activity without creating, modifying, signing, revoking or deleting."],[5085,"**Authenticates and authorizes** callers: operators for the API, and devices for the download routes. A device must only reach its own download paths, so match the device certificate to the `:deviceID` (or `:controllerId`) in the path."],[686,"**Authorities:** `ca.create`, `ca.import`, `ca.reissue` and `ca.delete`."],[601,"**Authorization administration**: manages principals, policies and grants. It should belong to a small, separate group."],[5766,"**Authorization.** Sign in as the bootstrap principal and confirm administration works, check that an identity without permissions receives `403`, and review client and integration permissions."],[3337,"**Automatic rollout.** With `auto: true`, hawkBit advances the groups itself once `approval_threshold` is met. The service does not run an event consumer in this mode. The thresholds are set on every rollout group, and hawkBit evaluates both percentages **per group**, not against all devices as native does. An unset or `0` threshold becomes 100, so an unset `error_threshold` never trips. The canary group always needs 100%. They apply with `auto` off too."],[605,"**Automation**: uses a technical account or a separate certificate, with permissions limited to the flow and resources it controls."],[3191,"**Batch mode** (**Fixed count** or **Percentage**), the batch size or percentage, and the switch **Auto rollout**"],[3190,"**Batching & rollout**"],[3150,"**Build here** builds the package from the pack's artifacts:"],[5228,"**Build names**: `encryption_alg_name` is passed to `swugenerator` as it is. Case is ignored, but the hyphens are required: `aes-256-gcm` works, `AES_256_GCM` is rejected. If the name differs from the algorithm stored with the key, the service logs a warning and uses the name from the request."],[3170,"**Build SWU from inputs**"],[1551,"**CA and profile:** prepare the issuer and certificate rules in PKI."],[2417,"**CA Certificate Expiration**: for example, one year from creation."],[2415,"**CA Name**: `Acme Evaluation Root CA`."],[670,"**Call Timeout** defaults to 10 seconds. The console rejects a value above 30 seconds with `Webhook timeout must be at most 30s.`; the API clamps the same value to the cap instead (see Calls are bounded)."],[679,"**Calls are bounded.** A call defaults to a 10 second timeout; a configured `call_timeout` is capped at 30 seconds. Values above the cap are clamped, not rejected."],[3186,"**Campaign basics**"],[3187,"**Campaign name (optional)**, **Distribution set**. **Device group** and **Driver** are inherited and read-only. **Workflow**: on native, every registered WFX workflow (Direct, Phased, Download-Install...), with a warning when it does not fit the driver. On hawkBit the field is **Install mode**: **Forced** (the device installs as soon as it polls) or **Soft** (the device may confirm or postpone)"],[3123,"**Campaigns**"],[1522,"**Cause:** Every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) triggers a `CreateProvisioningTemplate` attempt for the template named after the DMS ID, and the call is not idempotent: when the template already exists, it fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in `lamassu-dlq`. The connector’s later steps are skipped."],[1507,"**Cause:** Most commonly the certificate was provisioned in JITP mode. JITP certificates lack the connector's metadata key and are outside the state synchronization mechanism. If the DMS is in `auto` mode, it may indicate the initial provisioning did not complete correctly and the AWS ARN was not stored in the certificate metadata."],[1504,"**Cause:** The connector is not running or is not subscribed to the event bus. It can also indicate incorrect IAM credentials, insufficient permissions or that the metadata key was written with a wrong `connector_id`."],[1510,"**Cause:** The device uses an MQTT client ID different from the certificate's CN. The forced disconnect opens a connection using the CN as client ID; if the firmware uses another identifier, the disconnect does not affect that session."],[1513,"**Cause:** The MQTT client ID does not match the ThingName, which is always the CN of the certificate issued by Lamassu."],[1519,"**Cause:** The shadow feature is disabled in the DMS configuration, the connector's credentials lack IoT data-plane permissions, or the firmware reads a different shadow than the configured one."],[1516,"**Cause:** This is expected behavior while AWS executes the JITP template on the device's first connection."],[1103,"**Certificate and webhook:** requires both controls in the configured operation."],[811,"**Certificate History** relates previous and current versions. Replacing the slot does not by itself revoke every previous version."],[687,"**Certificates:** `ca.sign.certificate` and `certificate.delete`."],[767,"**Certification Authority (CA).** Signs certificates and establishes the chain of trust."],[5728,"**Check AMQP.** If you set `amqp.tls: true`, also set `amqp.port` to the broker's TLS listener (commonly `5671`), or set `amqp.tls: false` to stay on plaintext."],[1101,"**Client certificate:** useful when each device has an initial credential whose key it controls."],[2545,"**Client:** check cache, policy for expired or unavailable evidence, critical IDP extension and rejection of a new connection."],[1553,"**Client:** prepare the initial credential, server trust, and key generation or custody."],[2303,"**Common Name (CN)**: main name of the identity."],[3143,"**Compatibility**"],[14,"**Compute:** a `t3.large` instance at minimum. Use `t3.xlarge` or higher when the load requires it."],[5765,"**Connectivity.** `helm test \"$RELEASE\" -n $NAMESPACE --logs` passes; the hook checks the CA, DMS Manager, Device Manager and VA health endpoints and the UI response."],[1555,"**Continuity:** configure renewal, successor installation and recovery according to DMS policy."],[5727,"**Convert `services.connectors` to a map** keyed by connector ID, each entry with its `type` and `image`:"],[5741,"**Copy the data, align ownership and verify the copy.** The second command must print `copy verified`; any `diff` output means the copy is incomplete, so do not continue:"],[5237,"**Cost.** A per-device build runs one `swugenerator` process per device, in parallel up to the CPU available to the container. On a 100 MB artifact, one device costs roughly half a second of encryption, plus about three seconds more if the sw-description asks for gzip compression. Size the container accordingly: see Resource sizing. Builds of the same pack are serialized: a second build or version bump while one runs answers `409`."],[2306,"**Country (C)**: two-letter country code."],[5236,"**Coverage.** A device with no `firmware-encryption` key is skipped (the reason reads `no firmware-encryption key assigned` for `.swu` builds). The build still succeeds if at least one device was covered, and the pack records the coverage: read `built_device_count`, `total_device_count` and `uncovered_devices` on it. A launch on a pack with uncovered devices is refused with `400`, and there is no override: assign the missing keys and build again. If no device was covered, the build itself fails."],[3891,"**CPU** is the lever on per-device build time. A build runs one `swugenerator` per device, and the parallelism is derived from the container's CPU limit, not the node's cores: a `500m` limit collapses it to half a core. The chart's default of `2000m` is a starting point for groups in the hundreds."],[2842,"**Create and add** (GORM `AutoMigrate`). Missing tables and columns are created. It never drops or renames anything."],[2544,"**CRL:** compare `latest_crl`, number and timestamps with the downloaded list. Check the serial in its contents. If unchanged, review `regenerate_on_revoke`, event delivery, jobs, signing and storage with the deployment operator."],[2241,"**Crypto Engine**: cryptographic engine keeping the key."],[2264,"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the imported key."],[2254,"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the key."],[2276,"**Crypto Engine**: engine where the key resides."],[766,"**Cryptographic engine.** Generates or safeguards keys and executes private-key operations without exposing key material to the rest of the services."],[3108,"**Dashboard**"],[2843,"**Data migrations.** Standalone modules (`software_module_definition` rows move into the module and artifact tables, keeping IDs and files, and the old table is dropped), device assignments (rebuilt from the legacy launch columns), and approval-threshold semantics."],[5760,"**Databases.** Each service reads the `pki` database with its own schema, `authz` and `wfx` data are reachable, and the migration status is registered per schema, including the CA unique-ID and KMS composite-key migrations."],[3176,"**Decide later**"],[807,"**Decommissioned**"],[2418,"**Default End-Entity Certificate Issuance Expiration**: for example, 90 days, within the CA's lifetime."],[2249,"**Delete Key** to remove the key from the system."],[5724,"**Delete the gateway token validation blocks.** Remove `auth.oidc.apiGateway` and `auth.authorization` once their settings are in `services.authz`:"],[978,"**Delete** removes the DMS record and configuration; it does not itself cascade revocation across every device and certificate. Migrate dependencies and retain audit references before retirement."],[3174,"**Deliver files unchanged**"],[812,"**Device Event Timeline** helps reconstruct registration, provisioning, renewal and status changes. A change may be saved even if event persistence fails; also inspect the record and certificates."],[5768,"**Device events.** `GET /v1/devices/:id/events` returns the historical events of a device that had them."],[3135,"**Device Group**"],[1673,"**Device ID**: `device-001`."],[3207,"**Device Jobs**: one row per device with its job state, a **Refresh Jobs** button and, per row, a **Workflow** button that opens the device's timeline. For the Phased workflow, a **Next** button and a strip of transitions let you confirm steps, which is how a `phased` campaign moves on."],[768,"**Device Management Service (DMS).** Defines how the devices of a fleet are registered, authenticated and renewed."],[603,"**Device registration**: operates devices and DMSs without receiving permissions over keys or authorization configuration."],[769,"**Device.** Keeps its private key and presents the certificate to prove its identity."],[1540,"**Device**"],[690,"**Devices:** `device.create`, `device.identity.update` and `device.status.update`."],[3893,"**Disk.** Each in-flight upload is written to a temporary file under `TMPDIR` before it is stored, so overlapping uploads need that much free disk. Build scratch also uses `update_files_base_dir` (`/data` in the chart). Give the volume room for several simultaneous uploads (the umbrella example uses `50Gi`). The chart exposes no environment values, so setting `TMPDIR` to the volume needs a patched or wrapped chart."],[2090,"**Distributed CAs:** form the content the device obtains through EST `/cacerts`. You can include the service TLS certificate, the enrollment CA and an explicit list of managed CAs. Including the issuer does not automatically add all its ancestors."],[3117,"**Distribution Set**"],[1552,"**DMS policy:** define how the device is admitted, how requests authenticate and which trust is distributed."],[1544,"**DMS**"],[3139,"**Driver**"],[976,"**Edit** changes name, settings and metadata. Updates go through the DMS API. `PUT /v1/dms/{id}` always targets the DMS identified by the URL path: the body carries no `id` field, and any `id` or `creation_ts` in it is silently ignored — authorization is evaluated against the same URL identifier. The body must include a non-empty `name` and a `settings` object; missing either returns HTTP 400 and leaves the stored DMS unchanged. `name` and `settings` are always full replacements, so read the DMS before sending API edits to avoid losing fields. `metadata` is optional: omitting it or sending `null` keeps the stored metadata, a supplied object replaces it entirely, and `{}` clears it (a later GET returns an empty object, not `null`). Old-style bodies that carry the full DMS object keep working while they include `name` and `settings`; unknown fields are ignored. Updates reject an empty `name` with HTTP 400: a DMS whose stored name was zeroed by an earlier update must be renamed before any client round-trips it — the AWS IoT connector’s JITP writeback included. If the object does not include the AWS IoT connector key (`lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`), the connector ignores the DMS's later events."],[2309,"**Email Address**: contact email."],[651,"**Email** sends alerts to a person or an operational list. It only requires the destination address. Event content is rendered as HTML-escaped text, never as markup."],[5729,"**Enable the CA-to-KMS migration** if your CA keys predate the KMS:"],[3159,"**Encryption**, **Mode**"],[2542,"**Endpoint:** inspect AIA and CDP. Query those URLs from the consumer’s network; an old route or incorrect issuer can explain a discrepancy."],[2088,"**Enrollment CA:** signs the new identities."],[689,"**Enrollment:** `dms.create`, `dms.update`, `dms.enroll` and `dms.reenroll`."],[3202,"**Execute next batch** sends the next batch."],[5763,"**Existing CRLs are served.** `GET /crl/<ca-ski>` on VA returns a previously issued CRL, and new CRLs are issued."],[5762,"**Existing KMS keys work.** Issue a certificate from a CA that existed before the upgrade. None of the other checks reads a KMS key, so this is the only one that proves the key material was copied."],[803,"**Expired**"],[801,"**Expiring Soon**"],[5735,"**Find the new StatefulSets and PVCs.** Their names depend on the release name and any `nameOverride` or `fullnameOverride`; the PVC name is `<volumeClaimTemplate>-<StatefulSet name>-0`:"],[1546,"**Fleet**"],[2247,"**Generate CSR** to create a PKCS#10 request with the selected key."],[1874,"**Generate Key & CSR in Browser** is useful for a manual operation or a quick test. The browser generates the key and you must download it at the end."],[3232,"**Generate SWU** and **Download SWU** (or **Generate Package** and **Download Package**)"],[538,"**Global actions** do not need a resource identifier. `create`, `import` or access to a console section are common examples."],[977,"**Go to DMS owned devices** restricts inventory by owner. **Show/Edit Metadata** contains operational and integration data; consult the integration guide before changing it. &#x2A;*EST (RFC-7030)** helps locate URLs."],[3504,"**hawkBit mode only**: the pack's composition, as built catalog modules. Native ignores it"],[2723,"**hawkbit**: each module has its *own deliverable*, built in the module catalog before the pack exists. Only built modules can be linked into a pack, and a linked module is frozen. hawkBit hands every module's artifacts to the device, so a pack with several modules installs as several independent SWUpdate transactions. SWUpdate cannot roll back across several `.swu` files."],[672,"**HTTP Authentication** selects `No Authentication` (the default), `API Key` (API key value and Header Name, `X-API-Key` by default), `OIDC Client Credentials` (Client ID, Client Secret and Well-Known URL) or `Mutual TLS` (Client Certificate and Client Private Key pasted as PEM). A stored private key stays hidden until you select **Show**."],[1675,"**Icon** and **Tags**: optional classification."],[1542,"**Identity**"],[1835,"**Import a CA with its private key:** incorporate an external authority that Lamassu can issue with."],[1836,"**Import only the certificate:** register public material. If Lamassu finds its key in the KMS, it can associate it; otherwise, the CA has no usable key for issuance."],[971,"**Include Enrollment CA**"],[969,"**Include Lamassu System CA**"],[3212,"**Information**"],[5739,"**Inspect before writing** so you do not clobber data the new pods may already have written:"],[3242,"**Install mode**: **Forced** or **Soft**"],[3217,"**Installed Distribution Sets** with their artifacts, and the **Pack Update History**"],[1943,"**Intermediate CA**\\\n`Sign as CA`, `CertSign` and `CRLSign`, validity longer than its end-entity certificates and stricter cryptographic constraints."],[682,"**Invalid configurations are rejected at subscribe time.** A channel configuration that would fail on every delivery returns HTTP 400 with a body that starts with `struct validation error:`, and the subscription is not created."],[1724,"**IoT Fleets**"],[665,"**JavaScript** lets you express custom logic. Each filter runs for at most 100 milliseconds; a filter that exceeds this limit fails the condition and the notification is skipped with the error `javascript filter execution timed out`."],[3218,"**Jobs**"],[663,"**JSON Path** evaluates one or more specific fields."],[664,"**JSON Schema** accepts only payloads with a particular structure."],[2273,"**Key Identifier**: unique system identifier. Lamassu uses PKCS11-based ID formats."],[2263,"**Key Name**: descriptive name within Lamassu IoT."],[2272,"**Key Name**: descriptive name."],[2253,"**Key Name**: unique and descriptive name."],[5235,"**Key purposes.** The conventional purposes are `firmware-encryption`, `config-encryption` and `mac-verification`. The purpose is a placeholder: any value is accepted, and a binding with an empty purpose acts as a wildcard that matches any lookup. This is intentional while per-purpose keys are rolled out. An exact (case-insensitive) match wins over a wildcard, and among equals the most recent assignment wins. Expired or revoked bindings are skipped."],[2255,"**Key Type** and **Key Size**: algorithm and size or curve."],[692,"**Keys:** `kms.create`, `kms.import`, `kms.sign` and `kms.delete`."],[5761,"**KMS and VA start cleanly.** Their logs show no `no such file or directory` or `code=NotFound` errors."],[5752,"**KMS or VA pods crash with `no such file or directory` or `code=NotFound` right after the upgrade.** The pods are bound to the new, empty PVCs and the data migration has not run yet, or was copied into the wrong PVC. Follow step 4 and check that all four claims exist."],[3877,"**Leader election.** Leave `leaderElection.enabled: true`. The WFX event consumer advances a campaign's batches on every job completion, and the trigger starts due scheduled launches and repairs interrupted batches. With several replicas and no election, each one counts the same completions and releases the next batch before the current one succeeded."],[5764,"**Legacy CA keys.** If the CA-to-KMS migration ran, review its report and warnings and confirm the expected keys appear in the KMS."],[2308,"**Locality (L)**: city or locality."],[669,"**Log Level** selects `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error`; `info` is the default."],[973,"**Managed CAs**"],[3892,"**Memory.** Uploads, downloads and non-encrypting builds are streamed, so memory does not grow with the file (the maintainers measured a peak of about 150 MiB resident for a 10 GiB upload and for a 10 GiB download). Encrypting needs the file in memory: `swugenerator` reads an artifact into memory to encrypt it, AES-GCM of `non-swu` packages and `raw` modules works on one buffer, and a store with at-rest encryption enabled decrypts whole objects in memory. Size memory for the largest file you encrypt times the concurrent builds. In `hawkbit` mode, `build_memory_limit_mb` refuses such a build before the pod is killed."],[2286,"**Message Type**: `Raw` for clear data or `Digest` for a precomputed hash."],[2293,"**Message Type**: `Raw` or `Digest`."],[2288,"**Message**: content to sign."],[2295,"**Message**: original message or hash."],[2257,"**Metadata**: additional information for advanced uses."],[2266,"**Metadata**: additional optional information."],[652,"**Microsoft Teams** posts the notification to a channel through its webhook URL."],[2836,"**Modules** and **artifacts** are separate: an artifact is a file in the global catalog, and `distribution_set_artifact` links it to a pack version (and optionally to one module). A module whose `distribution_set_id` is empty is a catalog module that no pack holds yet."],[1941,"**mTLS device**\\\nEnd-entity certificate, short validity, `DigitalSignature`, `ClientAuth`, subject controlled by the DMS and SAN allowed when it identifies the device."],[3131,"**Name**"],[2237,"**Name**: descriptive name of the key."],[2722,"**native**: modules are *build inputs*. The pack builds one `.swu` from all of them, and a launch sends each device a single download address."],[16,"**Network:** HTTPS access from the necessary consumers, restricted to trusted networks."],[1834,"**New CA from the KMS:** reuse a key already registered in the platform."],[1833,"**New CA with a new key:** create a root of trust from scratch. Lamassu generates and keeps the key pair."],[795,"**No Identity**"],[5586,"**No injected scheduling rules.** The chart no longer adds pod anti-affinity or topology spread constraints on its own."],[5067,"**No roles or per-group permissions.** The service only does structural checks (see What the service checks). The gateway must authorize."],[4668,"**No**"],[4674,"**No**"],[4677,"**No**"],[4680,"**No**"],[4683,"**No**"],[4707,"**No**"],[4712,"**No**"],[4718,"**No**"],[4689,"**No** (poll)"],[1104,"**NO\\_AUTH:** does not establish requester identity; appropriate only when admission is deliberately controlled outside that DMS."],[5758,"**Nobody can log in after the upgrade.** Check that `services.authz.jwkUrl` is reachable from the `authz` pod, that `services.authz.bootstrap` matches a role your OIDC provider actually assigns, and that the database job completed."],[5584,"**Non-root containers.** Every workload runs as the numeric user `65532:65532` with a `RuntimeDefault` seccomp profile and dropped capabilities. Pods do not mount the service-account token by default, and the chart creates a dedicated ServiceAccount."],[5065,"**None in the service.** Every route is open to anyone who can reach the port. The gateway must authenticate."],[662,"**None** notifies all instances of the event."],[5210,"**None**: send neither."],[3342,"**Not available.** Per-device encryption, custom workflows (`GET /workflows` returns an empty list), editing a running strategy, marking a launch complete, pushed job events (progress is polled), set-level prebuilt upload, and building a pack directly. `POST .../swu` answers `501`:"],[5069,"**Not implemented.** The service always serves plain HTTP."],[2355,"**OCSP Check** shows what Lamassu returns for a selected certificate. Complete the check from the consumer’s network and trust store: a console query does not establish its validation configuration."],[2543,"**OCSP:** save a fresh response, verify signature and timestamps, and observe status. An HTTP error for an absent serial does not mean `Good` or guarantee a signed `Unknown`."],[3194,"**Optional**"],[2304,"**Organization (O)**: organization or company."],[2305,"**Organizational Unit (OU)**: department or unit."],[3221,"**OTA Dashboard**, **OTA Devices** and **OTA Device Groups** show version compliance: which devices are up to date, behind a declared target, or have no target. A group's page also lists the version each device runs against the group's released version, with an **Update to vX** button for the outdated ones. Without version-compliance support on the backend they still list devices and campaigns, but target versions show as **No target** or an em dash, with the alert \"Target versions unavailable on this backend\"."],[3114,"**OTA Device Groups**"],[3111,"**OTA Devices**"],[3235,"**Overview**, **Software Modules**, **Contents**, **Manifest**, **Version History** (composition is supported, so there is no pack-level **Artifacts** tab)"],[3206,"**Overview**: identity, preconditions, and the **Rollout Configuration** (workflow or install mode, rollout type and value, auto mode, test device). **Edit** on the rollout value works only where the backend supports editing a running strategy."],[3216,"**Package Inventory**"],[3339,"**Pause and resume.** `pause` and `resume` map to hawkBit's rollout pause and resume, but only a pause an operator made can be resumed. hawkBit already holds the rollout between groups when `auto` is off, so `pause` then answers `400` (\"Rollout can only be paused in state running but current state is paused\") and `resume` answers `400` (\"not paused; use Execute to release the next batch\"). Use `rollout` to release the next group."],[2294,"**Payload Encoding**: encoding of the message."],[2287,"**Payload Encoding**: input encoding, such as UTF-8, Hex or Base64."],[5209,"**Per-device**: send `encryption_alg_name` **without** `user` or `encryption_key_name`. This holds for `.../package` too: sending the algorithm is what selects per-device mode, and omitting it builds an unencrypted package."],[602,"**PKI operation**: administers CAs, profiles and certificates, but not necessarily who gets access."],[1721,"**PKI**"],[2267,"**Private Key (PEM)**: private key in PEM format."],[688,"**Profiles:** `profile.issuance.create`, `profile.issuance.update` and `profile.issuance.delete`."],[2240,"**Public/Private**: whether Lamassu manages the full pair or only the public key."],[3138,"**Raw File** (no restart), **Firmware** (restart), **Both** or **Other Type**"],[5744,"**Re-run the step 3 `helm upgrade` command.** The KMS and VA pods now start against the copied data, so `--wait` succeeds and the release moves from `failed` to `deployed`."],[5703,"**Receiver certificate verification.** A config with `validate_server_cert: false` becomes `insecure_skip_verify: true`, so receiver verification stays skipped until that field is set to `false`. Correct it on every migrated config: the webhook authorization channel carries the API key or bearer token, and skipping verification exposes those credentials to a network-positioned attacker. Keep `insecure_skip_verify: true` only when the receiver certificate is deliberately unverifiable."],[5770,"**Receiver verification.** `insecure_skip_verify` is `false`, unless the receiver certificate is intentionally unverified."],[5737,"**Record the replica counts, scale the new StatefulSets down and wait for every pod that uses the claims to exit**, so nothing writes to either volume while you copy. Helm removes the 3.8.0 `kms` and `va` StatefulSets during the upgrade, but their pods `kms-0` and `va-0` can still be terminating and holding the old claims. While they run, the helper pod either cannot attach the claims or copies a volume that is still being written. The `wait` returns at once for pods that are already gone:"],[2541,"**Record:** read the correct certificate again by serial and issuer. Confirm status, reason and time; expired or permanently revoked records reject further transitions."],[678,"**Redirects are not followed.** A `3xx` response fails with `unexpected status code: <n>: redirects are not followed`. Configure `webhook_url` with the final absolute `http` or `https` URL: an `http://` address that an ingress redirects to `https://` stops working."],[1674,"**Registration Authority**: the DMS you just prepared; this label identifies the service fulfilling the registration authority role."],[1781,"**Reissuing a CA** creates a new certificate over the existing key. Lamassu generates a CSR with that key, self-signs it if it is a root or asks the parent CA to sign it if it is subordinate. The new certificate becomes active and the CA points to its serial number; the old and new certificates are linked through metadata."],[2244,"**Related Entities**: certificates or other entities linked to that key."],[5583,"**Release-scoped resource names.** Every chart-managed resource is named `<release>-lamassu-<component>` instead of the fixed names used in 3.8.0 (`kms`, `va`, `ca`, ...). With a release named `prod` the StatefulSets become `prod-lamassu-kms` and `prod-lamassu-va`. The `nameOverride` and `fullnameOverride` values change the prefix."],[5743,"**Remove the helper and restore the recorded replica counts:**"],[5726,"**Remove the per-service identity overrides** `services.kms.securityContext.runAsGroup` and `services.authz.securityContext.runAsUser`/`runAsGroup`."],[5725,"**Rename `toolbox` to `connectivityTest`:**"],[2841,"**Rename step** (`MigrateDistributionSetNaming`). Databases created before the \"update pack\" → \"distribution set\" rename have their tables and columns renamed. It runs before step 2 so that the new tables are not created empty beside the old ones."],[799,"**Renewal Pending**"],[2047,"**Renewal** creates a successor certificate for an identity that remains valid. **Revocation** communicates that the current certificate must no longer be accepted. Renew to maintain continuity; revoke in response to compromise, retirement or a change that invalidates the identity."],[1520,"**Resolution:** Check that `shadow_config.enable` is `true` in the connector metadata for the corresponding DMS. If a Named Shadow is used, verify `shadow_config.shadow_name` holds the correct name and that the firmware reads that Named Shadow and not the Classic Shadow. Confirm the connector's credentials have `iotdata:GetThingShadow` and `iotdata:UpdateThingShadow` permissions."],[1523,"**Resolution:** Check that the template named with the DMS ID exists in AWS IoT Core and that JITP enrollment works. If the JITP configuration was changed, apply it by hand to the AWS template."],[1517,"**Resolution:** Firmware must implement retries with exponential backoff for the first connection. If the failure persists beyond a few minutes, verify the IAM role configured in `jitp_config.provisioning_role_arn` exists in the account and has permissions to create Things, register certificates and attach policies."],[1508,"**Resolution:** For JITP certificates, migrate the DMS to `auto` mode and re-provision the affected devices. For `auto` mode certificates, verify the certificate has the AWS ARN stored in its metadata; if not, repeat the provisioning."],[1511,"**Resolution:** Make sure the firmware uses exactly the certificate's CN as the MQTT client identifier. That CN matches the ThingName in AWS IoT Core."],[1505,"**Resolution:** Verify the connector process is active and the logs show no AWS communication errors. Check the metadata field was written under the exact key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`. If the error persists, the connector will update the status to `FAILED` with a descriptive message in the CA metadata."],[1514,"**Resolution:** Verify the firmware uses exactly the value of the CN field included in the enrollment CSR as the MQTT client identifier."],[5086,"**Restricts the dangerous routes** to the callers that need them (see below)."],[1554,"**Result:** request the certificate, install it and check its association with the device."],[2297,"**Result**: indicator of the validation result."],[5730,"**Review the remaining values:** overrides that pinned `services.ui.port` to `80`, the old `probes:` block, the TLS issuer, and explicit `affinity` and `topologySpreadConstraints` where your cluster needs them. Renamed and reshaped values describes each one."],[805,"**Revoked**"],[1782,"**Rotating a CA** creates a successor key and authority. It is the right choice in the face of compromise, a cryptographic change, an engine migration or non-exportable key policies."],[3704,"**Rows are scoped to a hawkBit instance.** hawkBit ids repeat across servers and after its database is wiped, so the service tags the hawkBit it talks to with a marker (a distribution-set tag named `lamassu:instance`) and ignores rows that belong to another instance. Do not delete that tag."],[5738,"**Run a short-lived helper pod** that mounts all four claims. It runs as root so it can change file ownership, so pin its image by digest and do not mount the service-account token:"],[3192,"**Safety controls**"],[3188,"**Schedule**"],[3201,"**Send to test device** sends the canary first."],[3724,"**Shared key only.** A distribution set serves identical artifacts to every target, so there is nowhere to put a different payload per device. Use `native` mode for per-device encryption."],[3876,"**Shared storage.** The default is a `ReadWriteOnce` volume, which cannot be mounted by pods on different nodes, so it limits the deployment to one replica regardless of `replicaCount`. Point `config.artifacts`, `config.builds` and `config.symkms.storage` at S3 and set `persistence.enabled: false`. SymKMS keys also default to local storage, and would not be shared between replicas. The service logs a warning at start-up when build outputs are on local storage. sw-descriptions are stored in Postgres, so nothing local is needed by another replica."],[5208,"**Shared**: send `user`, `encryption_key_name` and `encryption_alg_name`. A `.swu` build is refused without the algorithm. A `.../package` build picks AES-GCM from the key length and only records the name you send."],[2248,"**Sign / Verify** to test cryptographic operations on the key."],[2289,"**Signature**: result of the operation, shown in hexadecimal."],[2296,"**Signature**: signature to check."],[3158,"**Signing key**: none (unsigned) or a KMS key. With a key, pick a **Method** and a **Certificate**"],[3157,"**Signing**"],[3120,"**Software Modules**"],[3705,"**Staged uploads are heavy.** `POST /updates/v1/artifacts` (the global artifact upload) reads the whole file into memory and keeps it in Postgres for 30 minutes unless a pack links it, and hawkBit mode refuses to link files to packs. For large files, always upload through the catalog route of the previous section, which streams to hawkBit."],[3189,"**Start as soon as it is executed** or **Start at a planned time** (date and time, in your browser's time zone)"],[3263,"**Start Campaign** is disabled"],[2307,"**State / Province (ST)**: state or province."],[3341,"**Static membership.** A rollout is built from the devices of the group when the launch is created. Devices added to the group later are not picked up (`launch_device_assignment` is unsupported)."],[15,"**Storage:** an EBS `gp3` volume of at least 50 GB, sized to your inventory and retention needs."],[3340,"**Strategy read-back.** The strategy shown for a launch is derived from the hawkBit rollout's groups, not stored. A launch created with a fixed batch of 2 for 4 devices reads back as `percentage` 50."],[2239,"**Strength**: visual indicator of cryptographic strength."],[5585,"**Strict values validation.** `values.schema.json` rejects unknown keys, so leftover 3.8.0 blocks fail `helm lint` and `helm upgrade` instead of being silently ignored."],[2310,"**Subject Alternative Names (SANs)**: alternative names or identifiers, such as DNS or IP."],[2416,"**Subject**: organization and country details for your evaluation."],[3703,"**sw-descriptions** live in Postgres, not in hawkBit metadata, because hawkBit caps a metadata value at 4000 characters. Descriptors written there by older versions are still read."],[3155,"**sw-descriptor**"],[5227,"**SymKMS key names**: matching ignores case, and `_` and `-` are interchangeable (`aes_256_gcm` is stored as `AES-256-GCM`). The separators of the name must still be there: `ascon128a` is not `ASCON-128A`. A key whose length does not match its algorithm is rejected when it is created."],[1506,"**Symptom:** A certificate's status is not synced to AWS after a revocation."],[1521,"**Symptom:** After editing a DMS in `jitp` mode, DMS update messages appear in `lamassu-dlq`, or JITP configuration changes have no effect in AWS."],[1503,"**Symptom:** The CA registration stays in `REQUESTED` indefinitely."],[1512,"**Symptom:** The device receives `CLIENT_ID_REJECTED` when connecting to AWS IoT Core."],[1518,"**Symptom:** The Device Shadow is not updated when it should be."],[1515,"**Symptom:** The device's first MQTT connection in JITP mode fails or times out."],[1509,"**Symptom:** The forced disconnect does not terminate the device's MQTT session after revocation."],[2274,"**Tags**: associated labels."],[2243,"**Tags**: labels for classification and search."],[2265,"**Tags**: labels for organization and search."],[2256,"**Tags**: metadata to classify the key."],[5084,"**Terminates TLS** between clients and the service."],[3195,"**Test device** (the canary, updated first), the set's **Preconditions** (read-only), **Notes**, **Weight**"],[5754,"**The helper pod stays `Pending` or `ContainerCreating` and `kubectl wait` times out.** Another pod still holds one of the claims; on block storage `kubectl describe pod pvc-migrate-helper` shows a `Multi-Attach error`. Check with `kubectl get pods -n $NAMESPACE` that `kms-0`, `va-0` and the new KMS and VA pods are gone, then re-run the wait."],[680,"**The response body is ignored.** Delivery checks the status code only, so the receiver may answer with any body."],[1460,"**The Thing name in AWS is the Common Name (CN) of the device's certificate.** This convention is fundamental for the firmware developer: the `/CN=` value the device includes in its CSR when enrolling becomes the ThingName it must identify itself with in all MQTT operations against AWS IoT Core."],[5757,"**The upgrade fails at the CA-to-KMS hook.** CA certificates that share a `subject_key_id` declare conflicting `engine_id`s. Resolve the conflict and re-run."],[5756,"**The upgrade fails at the database job.** The PostgreSQL role cannot create databases or schemas, or a schema migration aborted on duplicate CA IDs or missing `engine_id` values. Check the job logs, fix the privileges or the data, and re-run the upgrade."],[677,"**TLS is always verified.** The receiver certificate is checked against the system trust store. `insecure_skip_verify` is enforced as `false` — including when it is set to `true` — so self-signed or otherwise untrusted receivers fail delivery."],[1942,"**TLS server**\\\nEnd-entity certificate, `DigitalSignature`, `ServerAuth` and mandatory SAN with the names clients will use."],[5772,"**Trust set.** `est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas` holds the expected CAs, in particular those of the old CA during rotation overlaps."],[3137,"**Type**"],[2238,"**Type**: algorithm and key size, for example RSA 2048 or EC P-256."],[3214,"**Update Status**"],[3246,"**Update Status** tab"],[1875,"**Upload Existing CSR** is the right option when the key must remain on the target system. Lamassu receives the request but never the private key."],[3163,"**Upload pre-built** attaches a `.swu` you built elsewhere, up to the service's `max_artifact_size_mb` (10 GiB by default). It is stored and served unchanged, nothing is signed or encrypted for you, and the version becomes built and immutable."],[3172,"**Upload prebuilt SWU**"],[770,"**Validation Authority (VA).** Publishes the status of certificates through OCSP and CRL."],[2089,"**Validation CAs:** authenticate the client certificates used for enrollment or migration. During re-enrollment, the enrollment CA is always trusted and Lamassu then tries the validation CAs configured in the re-enrollment block; those are optional."],[691,"**Validation:** `va.role.crl.create`."],[3133,"**Version**"],[2246,"**View Details** to inspect metadata, identifiers and relationships."],[5771,"**Webhook delivery.** One enroll or re-enroll through each migrated webhook completes its authorization call."],[1102,"**Webhook:** useful when another authority knows inventory, credentials or entitlement to a requested ID."],[653,"**Webhook** delivers the event to an automation, SIEM or incident system via `POST` or `PUT`."],[5702,"**Webhooks with file references.** A mixed config, with a PEM certificate and a file-path key, is migrated to `mtls_options.cert_file` and `mtls_options.key_file`. Webhook delivery rejects those fields on every call, whatever the configured authentication mode, so every enroll and re-enroll that calls that webhook fails until the config is fixed. If the webhook uses mTLS, re-enter the certificate and key as Base64-encoded or plain PEM; if it does not, remove the leftover file fields. Metadata-only updates keep working in the meantime (`PUT` or `PATCH` on `/v1/dms/:id/metadata`). In a variant of the same problem, a stored config with file paths inside `mtls_options.cert_content` or `mtls_options.key_content` makes every full settings update (`POST /v1/dms` or `PUT /v1/dms/:id`) fail validation; settings are replaced wholesale, so resend the config with the certificate and key as PEM."],[5769,"**Webhooks without file fields.** For each DMS that uses a webhook for enrollment or re-enrollment, the `config` block carries `insecure_skip_verify` and the expected option block (`apikey_options`, `jwt_options` or `mtls_options`), with the certificate and key as 