ption=\"Keys, signatures and the manifest.\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption\" />"],[3074,"<Card title=\"Signing and encryption\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption\" description=\"Keys, algorithms and what the device must hold.\" />"],[1072,"<Card title=\"Troubleshooting\" description=\"Distinguish transport, policy, issuance and installation failures.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting\" />"],[3076,"<Card title=\"Troubleshooting\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/troubleshooting\" description=\"Updates not reaching devices, and other failures.\" />"],[1068,"<Card title=\"Trust distribution\" description=\"Update roots and chains with verified overlap.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution\" />"],[386,"<Card title=\"Upgrade from 3.8.0\" description=\"Upgrade an existing Helm release to 4.0.0: architecture changes, breaking changes, migration steps and validation.\" href=\"/docs/upgrades/3-8-0-to-4-0-0\" />"],[388,"<Card title=\"Use Fastlane\" description=\"Bootstrap a complete evaluation or lab environment with one script.\" href=\"/docs/deployment/self-hosted/fastlane\" />"],[2021,"<Card title=\"Validate with OCSP\" description=\"Query and verify a certificate’s signed status.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />"],[2885,"1"],[1613,"1 hour."],[3275,"1\\. Check the backend"],[3372,"1\\. Check the backend"],[3082,"1\\. Create a distribution set"],[3469,"1\\. Create the pack"],[202,"1\\. Install the Gateway prerequisites"],[2852,"1\\. Publish and build a pack"],[3284,"10\\. Check the result"],[5218,"16 bytes"],[5224,"16 bytes"],[2888,"2"],[3083,"2\\. Add files and build (native)"],[2853,"2\\. Create a launch"],[3276,"2\\. Create a module in the catalog"],[203,"2\\. Create a values file"],[3373,"2\\. Create the pack"],[3470,"2\\. Upload the sw-description and the artifact"],[5226,"20 bytes"],[5220,"24 bytes"],[2891,"3"],[5613,"3.8.0 deployments with per-service databases"],[5670,"3.8.0 did not pin a container identity, so existing files can carry any UID. Any volume that holds keys or CRLs must be owned by `65532:65532`, or at least readable (and, where needed, writable) by that identity."],[5577,"3.8.0 gave each service its own database. The 4.0.0 backend connects to a single PostgreSQL database named `pki` and isolates each service in a schema: `kms`, `ca`, `va`, `devicemanager`, `dmsmanager` and `alerts`. The `authz` service keeps its principals, grants and policies in a separate `authz` database (the development monolith uses an `authz` schema inside `pki` instead), and WFX keeps its own `wfx` database."],[5667,"3.8.0 had no authorization service, so a 3.8.0 values file has nothing to migrate from: you must add the `services.authz` configuration yourself. It needs the JWKS endpoint of your identity provider, the database mappings the authorization engine uses, and a bootstrap principal that matches how your provider assigns the administrator role. Without a matching principal nobody can administer the PKI."],[5532,"3.8.0 to 4.0.0"],[3472,"3\\. Build the deliverable"],[207,"3\\. Install Lamassu"],[2854,"3\\. Roll out a batch and update a device"],[3085,"3\\. Start a campaign"],[3277,"3\\. Upload the file and the sw-description"],[3374,"3\\. Upload the sw-description and the artifact"],[1615,"30 minutes / 10 minutes."],[5222,"32 bytes"],[2894,"4"],[5580,"4.0.0 removes the gateway policy. The `/api/*` routes reach the services directly, and each service authorizes every request through `authz`, which validates the token against the identity provider's JWKS endpoint and applies fine-grained, entity-level policies to CAs, certificates, devices, DMSs, KMS keys and VA roles. There is no implicit superuser: the first administrator comes from a bootstrap principal that you configure. The access control page documents principals, policies and the first administrator."],[3375,"4\\. Build the `.swu`"],[3278,"4\\. Build the module"],[3473,"4\\. Create the launch"],[2855,"4\\. React to a device finishing"],[3086,"4\\. Run and watch the campaign"],[208,"4\\. Verify the deployment"],[1183,"400 `struct validation error` creating or updating a DMS"],[1167,"400, CSR or format error"],[1165,"404 or missing APS"],[2897,"5"],[2856,"5\\. Build a per-device encrypted pack"],[3279,"5\\. Create the pack from the built module"],[3087,"5\\. Follow devices"],[3376,"5\\. Preview, then create the launch"],[3474,"5\\. Roll it out"],[2900,"6"],[2128,"6 days and 23 hours."],[2858,"6\\. Build a module and create a pack"],[3280,"6\\. Preview the launch, then create it"],[3377,"6\\. Roll out the canary"],[2903,"7"],[2125,"7 days."],[2859,"7\\. Launch and device update"],[3378,"7\\. Play the device"],[3281,"7\\. Start the rollout"],[2906,"8"],[3282,"8\\. Play the device (DDI)"],[3379,"8\\. Roll out the rest"],[3380,"9\\. Check the result"],[3283,"9\\. Release the next batches"],[66,"99.5 %"],[70,"99.9 %"],[74,"99.99 %"],[504,"A **grant** is the association between a principal and a policy. Removing the grant removes all permissions contributed by that policy, without modifying it for other principals."],[2837,"A **launch** (`launchtrack`) targets a group through one assignment row per device. The assignment moves `pending` → `active` → `completed` or `failed`, and `batch_index` records which batch dispatched it."],[3164,"A **non-swu** pack has **Generate Package** instead. It builds a `.tar.gz` of the selected artifacts, with optional signing and optional shared or per-device encryption (the cipher is AES-GCM), and it has an **Upload pre-built** tab too. **Download Package** fetches the result."],[2835,"A **pack** (`distribution_set`) is one row that moves through versions. Each built version is snapshotted into `distribution_set_version`, with its signed manifest."],[501,"A **policy** groups reusable permissions. It can be granted to several principals and can contain rules about Lamassu entities, rules about HTTP routes or both."],[500,"A **principal** connects a credential to Lamassu. It defines how to recognize an OIDC or X.509 identity and can be active or disabled."],[3233,"A **Software modules** button instead: build in the catalog"],[877,"A &#x2A;*Device Management Service (DMS)** configures admission and identity lifecycle for its devices. It acts as a registration authority; the CA selected in `Enrollment CA` signs certificates. This guide is for the policy administrator."],[2710,"A `.swu` built with swugenerator, optionally signed and encrypted"],[5412,"A `.swu` was asked to be signed without both settings"],[5472,"A `HEAD` request, an artifact download by ID in `native` mode, a store without `presign` for that block, or the signing failed (logged)"],[673,"A `http://` URL shows the console warning `Unencrypted HTTP connection` (see the Credentials over http\\:// callout below)."],[3070,"A `non-swu` launch looks stuck although the device finished"],[3607,"A `non-swu` launch runs the SWU states"],[5495,"A `non-swu` launch shows SWU states (`native` mode)"],[2870,"A `non-swu` pack is built with `POST .../package` into a `.tar.gz`, and a deliverable built elsewhere is attached with `POST .../swu/upload` or `.../package/upload`."],[3050,"A `phased` job has moves that WFX or an operator makes, and nothing in Lamassu Updates advances them: it stays at `CREATED` until someone confirms it through WFX (the dashboard's **Next** button does that). The launch endpoints `force-version` and `sync-to-latest` use `phased` for SWU packs unless you pass a `workflow`. The workflow files are in `wfx/workflow/dau/` of the repository. The WFX HTTP API (listing jobs, updating status) is documented by WFX."],[5492,"A `phased` launch stays at `CREATED` (`native` mode)"],[3352,"A `raw` module cannot go into a `swu` pack, and a `swu-prebuilt` module cannot go into a `non-swu` pack"],[2918,"A `ready` rollout is started, and nothing happens while its start time is in the future."],[527,"A `skip_authz` route and a `default_action: \"allow\"` prefix grant access without consulting any policy. Every identity that matches an active principal can reach what they open, including identities with no granted policies. A `skip_authz` route in one schema also opens a request that another schema maps to a grant-protected action. Review these settings whenever you add or upgrade an integrated service."],[3316,"A `swu-build` module refuses a finished `.swu` as input, and a `swu-prebuilt` module takes exactly one `.swu`."],[431,"A 500 indicates an internal failure, such as an unavailable database. The `{\"err\": ...}` body currently includes the text of the internal error, which can contain schema, table and constraint names and, on connection failures, the database host and user (not the password). Find the cause in the service log using the request time, and do not forward the body to third parties or show it to end users. This is a known information-disclosure defect: generic messages are planned for 500 responses, keeping the detail only in the service log (tracked in lamassuiot#814). The authorization connector also returns the internal error, in the `details.error` field. Some request errors, such as a key size the engine does not support, are also returned as 500."],[944,"A body with `config.auth_mode` set to `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls` must carry its corresponding option block (`apikey_options`, `jwt_options` or `mtls_options`) — and nothing else. Bodies with those auth modes carrying only `apikey`, `oidc` or `mtls` fail validation with 400, and `validate_server_cert` is always ignored: use `insecure_skip_verify`. Only bodies without option blocks are accepted as `noauth`, and their credentials are dropped."],[854,"A bodyless `204` does not satisfy the contract. Denial, transport error, uninterpretable response or timeout abort authorization. Combined mode validates the certificate first, then queries the webhook. See the webhook specification."],[3669,"A build **replaces** the modu