ly: if that object does not carry the connector key, the key is dropped and the connector silently skips the DMS's future events. Once the template is attached, when a device with a certificate issued by that CA connects to AWS IoT Core for the first time, AWS executes the template automatically: it creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it."],[259,"K3s"],[280,"K3s ServiceLB implements each `LoadBalancer` Service with pods that reserve the Service ports on the node. On a single node, only one Service can therefore own port 80 and one can own port 443. The default K3s Traefik Service may already hold both ports."],[611,"Keep `failOpen: false` unless a documented risk assessment justifies otherwise."],[894,"Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession."],[613,"Keep at least two recovery administrators and test their access periodically."],[2104,"Keep enough overlap to renew a disconnected fleet."],[1233,"Keep initial EST server trust separate from identity consumer trust."],[727,"keep the complete CloudEvent unmodified;"],[1682,"Keep the device identifier, owning DMS and serial number as references for the result."],[3855,"Keep the real values in a values file that is not committed (`-f secrets.values.yaml`), or generate it from your secret manager at deploy time."],[1130,"Keep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinated operation in the device store or configuration; avoid temporarily exposing a certificate with another version's key."],[2100,"Keep the root out of daily operations, or with very restricted access."],[1985,"Keep the serial number, issuer, revocation reason, query time and evidence timestamps. An existing session may need explicit closure or reauthentication in the consumer; publishing revocation does not automatically close it."],[5087,"Keep the service on a private network. Devices need to reach only the download routes and, in `native` mode, the WFX **client** listener. In `hawkbit` mode they talk to hawkBit (and to the signed-URL store) and not to this service, except for the artifact download route described in hawkBit mode."],[274,"Keep these two settings separate:"],[4961,"Keeps every key under a \"folder\" of the bucket. One bucket can hold several stores, hawkBit's artifacts included. A missing trailing `/` is added."],[2526,"Key"],[4766,"Key"],[4800,"Key"],[4829,"Key"],[4856,"Key"],[4880,"Key"],[4908,"Key"],[4938,"Key"],[4969,"Key"],[4986,"Key"],[5006,"Key"],[1691,"Key and certificate"],[750,"Key custody and cryptographic operations"],[1746,"Key custody, CA hierarchy, profiles and validation."],[2202,"Key inventory"],[5216,"Key length"],[1761,"Key rotation procedure"],[1890,"Key Usage and Extended Key Usage"],[2192,"Keys and cryptographic engines"],[717,"keys and KMS engines;"],[2587,"Keys only (artifact bytes live in hawkBit's artifact store)"],[1722,"Keys, authorities, profiles, certificates and publication of revocation status."],[384,"KMS deployments using the native PKCS#11 sidecar require Kubernetes 1.29 or newer. Prebuilt Lamassu container images are published for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`, and the KMS PKCS#11 client module (`p11-kit-client.so`) is installed at the target architecture's Debian library path: `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on amd64 and `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on arm64. The x86\\_64 path is retained as a compatibility alias for existing configurations."],[1963,"KU, EKU and constraints permit the specific operation."],[378,"Kubernetes 1.24 or newer"],[5664,"Kubernetes derives a StatefulSet's PVC name from its pod name (`<volumeClaimTemplate>-<pod-name>`). Renaming the `kms` and `va` StatefulSets means `helm upgrade` binds brand-new, empty PVCs instead of the existing ones. The KMS key material and VA CRLs are not deleted — the old PVCs are orphaned, not removed — but the upgraded pods fail with `no such file or directory` (KMS) or `code=NotFound` (V