],[5130,"Lamassu KMS"],[2041,"Lamassu persists four X.509 states:"],[1360,"Lamassu resource"],[54,"Lamassu SaaS offers the platform's capabilities in a managed environment. It is the right option when your team wants to focus on PKI and device identities, not on maintaining the infrastructure that supports them."],[1794,"Lamassu saves the CA’s revocation, then attempts to revoke child CAs and issued certificates with `CessationOfOperation`. Propagation continues recursively through child CAs. This is not a transaction over the whole hierarchy: some resources may have changed while other updates failed."],[342,"Lamassu services and Gateway API resources"],[2232,"Lamassu shows the enabled engines in the console."],[3684,"Lamassu Updates"],[3926,"Lamassu Updates"],[3931,"Lamassu Updates (also called the OTA Update Service) orchestrates over-the-air software updates for IoT fleets. You publish an update, choose which devices receive it, and watch the rollout. The service builds the package that SWUpdate clients install, and can sign and encrypt it."],[5061,"Lamassu Updates delegates authentication, authorization and TLS to the platform gateway in front of it. This page lists what the service itself enforces, what it does not, and what to configure around it. Read it before exposing the service beyond a trusted network."],[2560,"Lamassu Updates is one process that serves the Updates API (`/updates/v1`) and, embedded in it, SymKMS (`/symkms/v1`). It also answers `GET /health`. Behind the API there are two interchangeable backends. The `mode` setting picks one when the service starts. Clients, including the Lamassu dashboard, cannot tell them apart except through capabilities."],[3094,"Lamassu Updates is running and the dashboard can reach it. See Connect it to the service."],[4750,"Lamassu Updates reads a single YAML file at start-up. This page lists every key the code reads, its default, and which backend (`native` or `hawkbit`) uses it. Keys that appear in the example files but are not read are listed separately so you do not rely on them."],[506,"Lamassu uses two authorization paths depending on who implements the protected service. They share principals, policies and grants, but they do not interpret the rules the same way."],[1244,"Lamassu's connector for AWS IoT Core syncs device identities with the AWS platform, handling CA registration, Thing provisioning and certificate lifecycle automatically and event-driven."],[1271,"Lamassu's integration with AWS IoT Core bridges the PKI managed by Lamassu and AWS's connectivity platform. When a device obtains its certificate through the EST enrollment process, the connector ensures that certificate and its associated identity are recognized in AWS IoT Core without manual intervention. The result is that the device can authenticate directly to AWS with the same X.509 certificate Lamassu issued."],[528,"Lamassu's native services embed authorization in their middleware and know the entities they operate on: certificates, CAs, issuance profiles, keys, devices, DMSs or subscriptions, among others."],[4915,"Largest single uploaded file in MiB: artifact or prebuilt `.swu`. A larger multipart file gets `413` with the message `file \"<name>\" is <n> MiB; the maximum upload size is <m> MiB`. The HTTP body limit is this value plus 1 MiB. Values of `0` or less mean the default."],[139,"latest"],[4130,"Latest versions: `.../latest-distribution-sets`"],[4025,"Launch"],[8,"Launch and verify"],[2690,"Launch campaign"],[3696,"Launch campaign"],[2667,"Launch campaigns"],[4032,"Launch campaigns"],[4441,"Launch history"],[4564,"Launch one device to a version. Answers `202`"],[3601,"Launch stays `running` with no device progress (hawkBit)"],[3729,"Launch strategy"],[2644,"Launches and device assignments"],[2777,"Launches and devices"],[4120,"Launches: `/groups/:groupID/launch`"],[3125,"Launches: list, create, run"],[5166,"Layer"],[2596,"Leader election"],[5517,"Leader election is failing, or WFX events are