ause**, **Retry N failed** and **Cancel campaign** (it asks \"Cancel Campaign?\"). **Pause** stops further batches while devices already in flight finish; **Resume** continues. There is no button for marking a campaign complete: a native campaign completes by itself when no device is pending or in progress."],[3200,"The row's main button releases a batch:"],[5270,"The same as `/mac`, plus `mac` (hex)."],[5520,"The same background work, which runs on the leader"],[4967,"The same credentials read from files, for example a mounted Kubernetes Secret. Re-read every minute, so a rotated Secret needs no restart. Set both or neither, and not together with the inline pair."],[5682,"The same KMS migration deletes, with a `NOTICE`, aliases that equal an existing key ID, because identifiers now resolve as a key ID before an alias. Affected aliases are dropped, not redirected."],[1591,"The same root CA can issue it for this isolated evaluation. The operational identity will have a different key and certificate. The matching CN makes the example easier to read; initial certificate authentication does not itself bind the bootstrap CN to the requested ID. Review admission before applying the policy to a population."],[5275,"The same SymKMS routes can run as their own process (`cmd/symkms`) instead of inside the monolith. See Configuration."],[3288,"The same update as in the native guide, against the service in hawkBit mode. The management requests go to Lamassu Updates. The device side is a different protocol: devices poll hawkBit's DDI API, not this service and not WFX. The commands were run against a real hawkBit 1.1.0, and the responses are real, trimmed where long. Examples that say they were not run were checked against the code only."],[4606,"The same, with the data as an uploaded `file`"],[3236,"The same. The **Artifacts** tab appears instead only if the hawkBit server lacks the module types"],[3019,"The sample device in `docker-compose.yml` (`swupdate-device1`) shows the minimum configuration. It takes three variables: `DEVICE_NAME` (its ID), `WFX_URI` (the WFX **client** API, `http://custom-wfx:8080/api/wfx/v1` inside the Compose network, `localhost:9080` from the host) and `PORT`. The WFX management API on `9081` is for the service, not for devices."],[3426,"The schedule survives a restart and runs on whichever replica currently holds the leader role, so a scheduled launch is dispatched once even with several replicas."],[5133,"The scheme of `symkms_endpoint` (normally the service itself, over plain HTTP in the examples), with no credential. Raw key material crosses this connection: keep it on `localhost` or a private network."],[5137,"The scheme of `wfx_endpoint` (plain HTTP in the examples), to the management listener."],[2972,"The second call reports the active backend and what it supports. In native mode it looks like this (shortened):"],[1036,"The server generates a software key, rebuilds the CSR with that key and performs initial enrollment. The flag must be enabled and the algorithm permitted by the profile. This operation's key does not become a hardware key because the CA is hosted in an HSM."],[5272,"The server generates the nonce for GCM, CTR and Ascon: **a caller-supplied `iv` is refused** for those algorithms. Only CBC accepts one (16 bytes). The response carries the `iv` used (hex)."],[2338,"The service accepts:"],[2750,"The service also keeps a **device inventory**: which pack version each device carries now, with the artifacts that version's manifest declares, and the history of update attempts. Both are filled as the service's launches run."],[5397,"The service always sends `X-Principal-ID: admin-mode`, and the gateway in between rejects it"],[4828,"The service always serves plain HTTP. These keys exist in the configuration structs and in the example files but **are not read**, so setting them has no effect: `app.server.protocol`, `app.server.log_level`, `app.server.health_check` (and the `health_check_logging` of the examples), `app.server.cert_file`, `app.server.key_file` and `app.server.authentication.mutual_tls.*`. Terminate TLS in front of the service. See Security."],[3906,"The service does not export traces, metrics or logs. Its outgoing HTTP clients (the SymKMS client) and its database layer are instrumented with OpenTelemetry, but no tracer provider or exporter is configured, and there is no metrics endpoint. The `otel/` directory of the repository holds a local development stack (Grafana Alloy with OTLP receivers on 4317 and 4318, Loki, Tempo, Prometheus, Grafana; Jaeger in `otel/jaeger/`) that nothing feeds today. Until exporters are added, monitor the process log and `/health`, and watch Kubernetes events and Leases."],[5089,"The service does not forward the caller's identity. Calls to the Lamassu Device Manager and the KMS carry the fixed header `X-Principal-ID: admin-mode`, which the platform's authorization layer accepts for internal callers and which the platform gateway strips from external traffic. The value is not configurable, so point `dms_endpoint` and `kms_endpoint` at internal service addresses, not at a public gateway path that would reject or forward it. SymKMS calls from the service carry no credential."],[2991,"The service exits at start-up when run on the host"],[3781,"The service exports no traces, metrics or logs, and has no metrics endpoint. See Operations."],[5230,"The service generates the IV or nonce. In `native` mode the algorithm and, for shared-key packs, the IV are passed to the device in the WFX job. A per-device build draws a fresh IV for each device and does not record it in the job, so the job carries the algorithm only. The job fields are listed in Connect a device."],[3746,"The service has no authentication, authorization, TLS or mTLS. Use a gateway. See Security."],[3485,"The service has no built-in authentication, so these examples send none. Put a gateway that authenticates callers in front of it: see Security."],[3414,"The servi