fix it serves. Do not publish the store's console or S3 API with default credentials."],[4507,"The strategy update body takes `workflow_type`, `rollout_type`, `rollout_value` (greater than 0), `test_device_id`, `auto`, `approval_threshold` and `error_threshold`. It replaces all of them: an omitted field resets to its zero value, so send the whole strategy."],[675,"The summary in the Confirm step shows the authentication as `None`, `OIDC client credentials`, `API key` or `Mutual TLS`. The subscription details show the authentication, the API key header name (never the value), the OIDC issuer URL, mTLS as `Configured` and a fixed `TLS verification: Verified` row. The API key, client secret and private key are not shown in the subscription details."],[272,"the surrounding network already routes traffic for it to the cluster"],[5213,"The sw-description must mark the artifacts to encrypt with `encrypted = true;`. A per-device build is refused when no artifact is marked, because the key alone does nothing."],[3400,"The sw-description tells SWUpdate what to install. This minimal one installs a web page. See the sw-description rules in Publish and roll out an update."],[4289,"The sw-description text (the key has a hyphen)"],[2085,"The system that trusts the device validates the chain, dates, uses and revocation status."],[2236,"The table includes the following fields:"],[2781,"The tables Lamassu Updates keeps in Postgres, how they relate, and how the schema is created and migrated. This is a reference for operators and integrators who query the database, size it, or plan an upgrade."],[2772,"The tables the service keeps, how they relate, and how the schema is created and migrated."],[1449,"The tags AWS IoT Core applies to the registered CA certificate are `LMS.CA.ID`, `LMS.CA.SN` and `LMS.CA.CN`."],[2673,"The terms Lamassu Updates uses, and how they fit together. Read this page before the guides if \"update pack\", \"launch\" or \"module\" is new to you."],[412,"The test checks the health of CA, DMS Manager, Device Manager and VA, and verifies that the UI returns HTML. A passing test confirms basic connectivity, not a full issuance flow."],[2155,"The test should accept an allowed certificate absent from the list and reject it with `certificate revoked` after publishing and using the list containing its serial."],[1464,"The Thing name in the JITP template is derived from `AWS::IoT::Certificate::CommonName`, which corresponds to the device certificate's CN — the same convention as `auto` mode."],[459,"the time scope and affected resources;"],[5686,"The tool is idempotent: keys already in the KMS are skipped, so it can be run several times."],[5409,"The tool rejected its input. Its own message is in the service log"],[5117,"The TTL is short (default 60 s). It bounds when a GET can **start**: a transfer in progress finishes after expiry, and a device that retries or resumes goes back to the static endpoint for a fresh URL. A URL already signed for the same object is reused while it has `min_remaining` validity left (default `min(25s, ttl/2)`); it grants no more than a fresh one."],[3751,"The two backends differ in features. See Capabilities. Notably, `hawkbit` mode has no per-device encryption, no custom workflows, no editing of a launch strategy after creation, no early completion of a campaign, no adding devices to a running campaign, no push events and no set-level prebuilt upload. A `swu-prebuilt` catalog module does accept a finished `.swu`. `native` mode builds one deliverable per pack and has no per-module deliverables."],[3119,"The update packs of every group"],[2624,"The update server"],[4040,"The Updates API is served under `/updates/v1`, and SymKMS under `/symkms/v1`. Paths in this page are relative to `/updates/v1` unless they begin with `/health` or `/symkms`. Behind the Lamassu gateway the prefix is typically `/api/updates/v1`, which is what the dashboard calls."],[2955,"The Updates API, with SymKMS embedded"],[5714,"The upgrade runs in a fixed order: prepare the data and values, run `helm upgrade`, then copy the KMS and VA data into the new volumes. The copy must run after the upgrade, because the destination StatefulSets and PVCs do not exist before it. If you are not upgrading an existing release, go straight to Install with Helm."],[5469,"The URL expired before the device started the GET, the signed `Host` is not what the device used (`public_endpoint` wrong), the signing key cannot read that prefix, or clocks differ"],[2141,"The VA identifies each CRL by the CA’s SKI, and the SKI is derived from its key. Two CAs with the same key have the same SKI and share a single VA role and a single CRL. This happens when a CA is created on an existing KMS key, or when a CA is imported whose certificate declares an SKI already in use. Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. The first CA creates the role and the others keep it."],[2119,"The VA role stores configuration and metadata for the latest list published for a CA’s SKI. Generation requires a usable issuer key; do not assume an imported CA without a key has an available CRL."],[2145,"The VA scheduled job checks every role on each run and requests another list when the remaining validity of `latest_crl` is less than the interval between runs. The frequency is set in Helm with `services.va.job.crl.frequency` (default `0 * * * *`) and accepts a duration (`1h`) or a cron expression. A cron expression is converted into a constant interval equal to the time left until its next firing when the service starts, so it is not aligned with the clock. `refresh_interval` plays no part. Keep `validity` well above the interval: with a shorter validity, the list is regenerated on every cycle."],[2151,"The VA service exposes `GET /crl/{subject_key_id}` without management authorization. Use the **issuing CA’s** SKI, not the end-entity certificate’s SKI or serial. First inspect the certificate’s CDP:"],[5466,"The value has no scheme, or has a path"],[1180,"The value is neither Base64-encoded PEM nor plain PEM: it is a file path or Base64 of non-PEM data. Enter the certificate and key as Base64-encoded PEM, like certificates elsewhere in the API, or as plain PEM (see the webhook settings in DMS). The same message names `mtls_options.key_content` when the key is the offending value."],[118,"The value must be an address handled on the Kubernetes side of the traffic path. If a public EC2 address forwards through WireGuard to the cluster, pass the reachable cluster/VM address, not the EC2 public address."],[5403,"The version has no sw-description (each version needs its own)"],[3596,"The version is already built (create a new version), or another request is building the same pack"],[3431,"The version must be higher than the current one, and the pack keeps its id. The new version starts as `draft`. The artifact links and the module composition carry over, but the sw-description does not: upload it again. Then build with `selected_artifact_ids` (a build replaces the carried links with the ones you select), after uploading a new artifact version if the file changed."],[3574,"The version must be strictly higher than the current one. The new version starts unbuilt and carries the current artifacts forward."],[252,"the version upgrade guides and the notes in `charts/lamassu/CHANGELOG/` before every upgrade"],[3836,"The volume mounted at `/data`: build scratch and, by default, local update files."],[668,"The Webhook and Microsoft Teams channels show this configuration in the subscribe form, at the channel step, and again when editing a subscription; Email does not use it. The fields map to the optional `http_client` block described above:"],[1174,"The webhook answered with a redirect. Configure the final absolute `http` or `https` URL in the webhook config; redirects are never followed."],[917,"The webhook authentication modes read `external_webhook_settings` in the same `est_rfc7030_settings` block:"],[1184,"The webhook config of the operation that uses it is invalid. Review `external_webhook_settings` in DMS."],[1178,"The webhook config points to files (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`). For mTLS, enter the certificate and key as Base64-encoded PEM in `cert_content` and `key_content`, or as plain PEM; remove any other file field, including `ca_cert_file`, which has no inline equivalent (the receiver certificate must chain to the system trust store). See the webhook credentials guidance in DMS."],[852,"The webhook should verify credential, its relationship to `device_cn`, DMS, operation and admission policy. Forwarded headers are not themselves trusted evidence. **Lamassu-to-webhook authentication** is a separate relationship configured in the server’s HTTP client."],[177,"the well-known `lamassu`/`lamassu` password is fixed and permanent: it is never rotated and grants the `pki-admin` role, including non-interactive tokens through the `frontend` client"],[2733,"The WFX workflow: `direct` (no confirmation), `phased` (confirmation steps) or `download-install`. Use `download-install` for `non-swu` packs. Any other value is passed to WFX as a full workflow name, such as `wfx.workflow.dau.simple`. In hawkBit mode there is no WFX: `phased` becomes a hawkBit soft action (the device confirms or postpones) and every other value a forced action"],[3842,"The whole service configuration, rendered into a ConfigMap."],[3268,"The workflow does not match the pack's driver. SWU packs need **Direct** or **Phased**; Generic packs need **Download-Install**"],[2948,"Then check that `dms_endpoint` and `kms_endpoint` in `config-docker.yml` point at your Lamassu API (they default to `http://host.docker.internal:8080/api/...`)."],[3580,"Then continue at step 4. Details, including the delivery intents, are in hawkBit mode, and a full request sequence is in hawkBit curl guide."],[5746,"Then go through the validation checklist. Keep the old PVCs until it passes."],[197,"Then open `https://pki.example.com` and sign in with the initial user. If the Gateway is programmed but the URL is unreachable, follow the checks in Expose the Gateway."],[3149,"Then press **Generate SWU** in the header. The sheet **SWU deliverable** has two tabs."],[3921,"There are no migration files. At start-up the service renames, creates and migrates its own tables under a Postgres advisory lock, so replicas starting together do not race. Among others it renames the tables and columns of the former \"update pack\" vocabulary to the \"distribution set\" ones, folds the standalone-module table into the module tables, backfills device assignments and converts approval thresholds. See Data model."],[2840,"There are no migration files. In native mode the service creates and upgrades its own schema when it starts, holding a Postgres lock so that replicas starting together do not race. It runs these steps in order (hawkBit mode only creates its six tables):"],[1452,"There are three registration modes, and the choice among them depends on the fleet's provisioning strategy."],[1496,"There are two action types:"],[2173,"There is no API way out; it requires database intervention. Avoid it by adjusting the role before deleting the CA."],[3923,"There is no automated rollback, and migrations are one-way: an older image does not understand renamed tables. To go back, restore the database and the blob stores from the backup, and run the previous image."],[3196,"There is no device picker. A campaign targets every device of the pack's group, and the **Campaign summary** panel shows how many match and the estimated number of batches."],[237,"There is no implicit superuser. Create the role or group expected by `services.authz.bootstrap` in the OIDC provider and assign it to at least one administrator before exposing Lamassu."],[3750,"There is no rate limiting."],[671,"There is no TLS-verification switch for these channels, because the service always verifies the receiver certificate (see TLS is always verified)."],[3932,"There is one HTTP API and two interchangeable backends behind it, chosen with the `mode` setting. The backend decides which update server the devices talk to, so the systems around the service depend on it."],[3256,"These API features have no screen: marking a campaign complete (`/complete`), syncing all of a device's sets to the group's latest versions at once (`/sync-to-latest`), and declaring a group's latest version. Use the API for them. To push one set to one device, &#x2A;*Launch Update...** and the per-device **Update to vX** button on a group's page do it."],[523,"These are decisions of the schema, not of a policy: `skip_authz` removes the grant check for that route, even when another schema maps the same request to an action that requires a grant, and `default_action` only covers requests that no explicit route in any schema matches. Keep three rules in mind:"],[5108,"These are defence in depth, not a substitute for the gateway:"],[3398,"These are the addresses of the Docker Compose stack in the repository. Through a Lamassu gateway the API is usually `https://lamassu.example.com/api/updates/v1`, and every call then needs the credential your gateway requires, for example `-H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"`. The service itself does no authentication. See Security."],[2509,"These checks validate the issued material. Acceptance by a service also requires its trust, authorization and revocation configuration."],[2861,"These diagrams follow what the service does, step by step. Routes are relative to `/updates/v1`. For runnable commands see the native and hawkBit curl guides, and the API reference for every route and body."],[1944,"These patterns express design decisions. Enabling `Honor Extensions` does not require a CSR to contain SAN or validate its names; enforce that in the request workflow. A profile does not install trust or grant permissions in the consumer."],[1029,"These routes do not implement `/csrattrs` or `/fullcmc`. There is no native JWT EST authentication mode; a webhook can interpret a request credential according to its own contract."],[1671,"These settings prepare DMS policy. The next steps register and assign through the console; they do not send an EST request or test admission through that protocol."],[541,"They are not two equivalent controls applied twice. HTTP rules protect the perimeter of an integrated service; entity rules express permissions over the data model of the native services."],[1254,"Thing lifecycle"],[1382,"Thing lifecycle"],[1433,"Things only exist under `auto` mode; in `jitp` mode AWS creates the Thing itself when the device first connects. The Thing name is always the device certificate's CN:"],[3285,"Things that behave differently"],[2863,"Things to know about the build:"],[1073,"This block describes `main` code inspected at commit `8f42c47157f9a36bac3ed5caa3692aebebe19c4b`. The site catalog publishes `main (unstable)`; this behavior is not attributed to a stable release. Before using another version, check its contract, particularly independent re-enrollment authentication. Client checks and live-instance tests are distinguished in the revision plan."],[279,"This design uses one IP, one TLS entry point and one pair of ports. The chart already creates the required routes."],[1273,"This document is aimed both at the PKI administrator, who needs to configure the connector and register CAs, and at the device firmware developer, who needs to understand how Things are named, how the Device Shadow works and what happens when a certificate is revoked. For the firmware developer, the most important fact is that **the AWS Thing name (ThingName) always corresponds to the Common Name (CN) of the certificate issued by Lamassu**, regardless of the provisioning mode."],[2020,"This does not undo permanent revocation or a cascade started by revoking a CA."],[2351,"This example omits the nonce because the responder does not echo it. Verification relies on signature, response correspondence and timestamps; it does not demonstrate freshness through a nonce."],[557,"This example recognizes certificates whose CN starts with `factory-a-` and that are signed by the configured CA:"],[3421,"This example uses a second pack, `valve-panel`, created and built like `water-panel` (steps 2 to 4). Artifact names are global and a name that already exists must get a higher version, so give this one its own file name:"],[2299,"This flow is useful for:"],[1858,"This flow is useful when the key was previously created in an HSM, imported through BYOK or must be reused under a specific custody policy."],[1491,"This forced disconnect happens at the moment Lamassu records the status change. If the device tries to reconnect after the disconnect, AWS IoT Core will reject the connection because the certificate is already listed as `REVOKED` or `INACTIVE`."],[1952,"This guide brings together the checks needed when integrating a certificate consumer. You need the presented certificate, intermediates and roots that your application has deliberately chosen to trust. Lamassu publishes status; the consumer applies acceptance policy."],[841,"This guide connects the DMS administrator with device manufacturing and integration. Before requesting an operational identity, the client must authenticate the EST server and establish entitlement to request the intended ID."],[3013,"This guide describes the contract between a device and Lamassu Updates, for each backend. The device software is SWUpdate: this page covers what the device must send and receive, not SWUpdate's own installation and configuration, which its documentation describes. Use it when a launch is running and no device moves."],[2518,"This guide helps a PKI operator locate issuance and status publication failures. Keep the Lamassu version, operation, CA, issuer, serial, time and returned error. Do not include private keys or tokens