t an update."],[3480,"This guide takes one update from nothing to devices: create an update pack, attach an artifact and a `sw-description`, build the deliverable, create a launch (a campaign in the dashboard) and release it batch by batch. The requests use native mode. hawkBit mode builds and creates packs in a different order, described in hawkBit mode. To do the same through screens, follow Use the dashboard."],[3433,"This helper creates a pack, uploads a sw-description that marks its file `encrypted = true;`, and uploads the artifact. It prints the artifact id."],[630,"This is the expected behavior with `failOpen: false`. Restore the service, its PostgreSQL connection and its JWKS connectivity. Do not temporarily switch to `failOpen: true` without accepting that protected routes could become accessible without an authorization decision."],[3327,"This is the local setup. In a deployment where devices reach hawkBit through the Lamassu gateway, `hawkbit.ddi_url` is the gateway path (for example `https://lamassu.example.com/api/hawkbit-ddi`) and devices authenticate as the gateway requires, typically with their client certificate. See Connect a device."],[567,"This lets you express models like \"can operate the devices of this DMS\" without maintaining an individual list of every device. When a resource is added under that relation, it inherits the scope planned by the policy."],[1465,"This mode requires the `jitp_config.provisioning_role_arn` field to be configured with the ARN of an IAM role AWS IoT Core can assume to execute the template. If not configured, the connector uses the default `arn:aws:iam::{account}:role/JITPRole` and logs a warning. The administrator must ensure that role exists in the account and has the necessary permissions before enabling JITP mode."],[499,"This model lets you represent people, identity-provider groups, service accounts and devices without coupling authorization to a specific provider."],[3745,"This page collects what is unsupported, partial or surprising, so you can check it before you design a rollout. Each item points to the page that explains it."],[5165,"This page explains how Lamassu Updates protects what it delivers: which keys sign and encrypt a package, how to configure them, and what each backend supports. There are three independent layers."],[3801,"This page is for whoever runs the service: how to deploy it, what to put around it, how it behaves with several replicas, what to back up and how to upgrade. Configuration keys are documented in the configuration reference; the threat model is in Security."],[5571,"This page is organized in four parts. Read them in order before you upgrade production:"],[1658,"This path is intended for those starting to administer device identities. You will register `device-001` and assign an existing certificate to check the relationship between device, DMS and identity history."],[2491,"This path is intended for those starting to administer PKI or needing a first X.509 client identity. It ends with three files: a root CA certificate, an end-entity certificate and the client's private key."],[508,"This path protects APIs that join the platform through the Gateway but do not natively implement Lamassu's authorization SDK. Job Manager is the current example. The same pattern allows integrating other HTTP services as long as their routes are described in an `authz` HTTP schema."],[1913,"This preserves SAN, but does not establish that the requester is entitled to those DNS names, IP addresses, emails or URIs. Validate that authorization before issuing. OCSP and CRL locations come from the issuance service configuration; `Honor Extensions` does not copy CSR distribution points."],[1771,"This procedure is for the PKI operator replacing an authority in use. You need access to CAs, keys and profiles, a dependency inventory and coordination with trust-store and device owners. Before switching issuance, prepare a test cohort and decide the criterion for ending overlap."],[273,"This requests `192.168.1.50`; it does not claim the IP on your LAN or make it reachable."],[312,"This requires distinct SNI hostnames and cluster networking outside the Lamassu chart."],[1550,"This section connects PKI rules to the device client. Preparation follows this order:"],[2091,"This separation makes it possible to migrate a fleet: you temporarily accept certificates from the old hierarchy, issue with the new one and distribute both chains during the overlap."],[3962,"This service's Postgres, plus WFX"],[2290,"This tab checks whether a signature corresponds to the associated public key."],[2283,"This tab uses the private key to generate a digital signature over a message or an already computed *digest*."],[3227,"Those tiles, the status filter and column are hidden. A set has no build state of its own: it is ready when all its modules are built, and **Start Campaign** stays disabled until then"],[309,"Three independent `LoadBalancer` Services cannot all reserve the same node ports 80 and 443 through K3s ServiceLB. Use one shared Gateway, allocate distinct VIPs with another load-balancer implementation, or use different ports."],[1216,"Three trust relationships"],[5388,"Thresholds are percentages"],[2124,"Time from `thisUpdate` to `nextUpdate`."],[1203,"Timeout or error after issuance"],[1149,"Timeout, HTTP 500 or installation failure"],[4054,"Timeouts"],[4307,"Timestamps"],[365,"TLS"],[5068,"TLS and mTLS (inbound)"],[87,"TLS behavior"],[5070,"TLS to other systems"],[5187,"TLS towards the KMS: in `native` mode the service's KMS client skips certificate verification, and `sign_kms.sh` calls `curl -k`. In `hawkbit` mode the service's own KMS calls verify the certificate, but the SWU signer script still uses `curl -k`. Reach the KMS over an internal address you trust. See Security."],[204,"TLS with an existing Secret"],[1832,"To administer CAs, you need authority and key permissions, a reachable engine and a reviewed profile. An external consumer does not trust a CA merely because it appears in this inventory: agree root and chain distribution separately."],[5748,"To apply migrations by hand, `goose-lamassu` must run against the `pki` database with `search_path=<schema>` in the connection string. The binary validates only `search_path`, not the database name, so check `dbname=pki` before running it, and repeat for `kms`, `ca`, `va`, `devicemanager`, `dmsmanager` and `alerts`:"],[1276,"To avoid infinite loops, the connector ignores any event whose CloudEvent `source` field matches its own origin URI. This prevents the metadata writes the connector itself performs from re-triggering the same flow."],[2976,"To build `.swu` files outside Docker, install `swugenerator` into the Python environment that the config points at (the image does the same from `swugenerator/requirements.txt`):"],[3635,"To build deliverables in this mode, add the pieces you need on top. All are read once at start-up:"],[3779,"To build the Docker image yourself, the build context must contain the `lamassuiot/core` and `lamassuiot/sdk` checkouts next to `ota/`."],[3165,"To change a built pack, create a new version: use **New Version** in the pack list's row menu (the dialog has **Create Version**), or **New Version to Edit** on the pack's **Artifacts** tab. The new version must be higher than the current one, and it clears the built package."],[2356,"To check a change, save an earlier response, revoke a test certificate and request another response. The new query should reflect persisted status; compare timestamps to rule out reuse of an earlier response. If it fails, follow PKI troubleshooting."],[3103,"To compare both backends, the user menu has an **Updates backend** switch with the options **Auto**, **native** and **hawkbit**. It appears only in development builds, or when the dashboard container runs with `DEV_OPTIONS_ENABLED=true`. Choosing native or hawkbit overrides `LAMASSU_UPDATES_API` for that browser, shows an editable **Updates API base URL** field and reloads the page. The defaults are `http://localhost:10090` (native) and `http://localhost:10091` (hawkbit) on localhost, and `<origin>/api` and `<origin>/api/hawkbit` on any other host."],[3519,"To deliver a file you built elsewhere, upload it to `POST .../swu/upload` (or `.../package/upload`) as a multipart `file`. It is stored and served unchanged, so it must already be signed and encrypted the way your devices expect. Native mode only."],[1831,"To design a full chain or replace an authority in production, see CA hierarchy and rotation. If you need to standardize what each authority can sign, use Certificate profiles."],[2144,"To detect the problem, compare the SKI of the CAs in the inventory: two with the same SKI are affected. To fix it, create a CA with its own key and reissue its certificates; those already issued keep the old URL and are not corrected until reissued. A CA imported with an SKI already in use does not replace the existing one as CRL issuer, because the issuer is resolved from the role’s signer. However, its own certificates point to that same URL and its revocations are not published. Until Lamassu rejects these imports, restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with &#x2A;*Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same collision, so also avoid reusing keys across CAs. A per-CA CRL and the rejection of imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA are both proposed in lamassuiot#834, and neither is implemented yet."],[241,"To install a local checkout instead:"],[2511,"To manage a device identity in Lamassu, follow Manual registration. That path includes DMS preparation."],[2510,"To operate certificates, continue with Certificates and Validation."],[3333,"To report a failure, send `\"finished\":\"failure\"` in the `closed` feedback."],[608,"To revoke access immediately, disable the principal or revoke its grants. Disabling a principal keeps its configuration and associations for investigation or restoration, but it stops taking part in matching."],[3875,"To run more than one `native` replica:"],[3323,"To start the rollout later, add `scheduled_at` (RFC 3339) to the create body. hawkBit's own scheduler then starts it, and a `rollout` call before that time leaves the schedule alone. Not run here."],[1537,"To start with an existing certificate, use Register a device manually. You can then prepare enrollment and renewal for the next device."],[3418,"To track drift, declare the version devices should be on, then read compliance:"],[3575,"To update one device to an exact version without a campaign, use `POST $API/devices/<device-id>/force-version` with `{\"distribution_set_id\":\"<pack id>\",\"version\":\"1.1.0\"}`; in native mode it creates a one-device launch and defaults to the `phased` workflow for SWU packs (`download-install` for `non-swu`) unless you pass a `workflow`."],[1699,"To use the identity in AWS IoT Core, also prepare trust and permissions in the destination. Check the connection from the device after installing the required key, certificate and chain."],[4042,"Topic"],[1397,"Transition"],[1410,"Transition"],[1421,"Transition"],[1434,"Transition"],[5125,"Transport security"],[4727,"Treat a `501` on a route as authoritative: hide the action instead of retrying."],[1270,"Troubleshooting"],[2938,"Troubleshooting"],[3011,"Troubleshooting"],[3090,"Troubleshooting"],[3286,"Troubleshooting"],[3368,"Troubleshooting"],[3391,"Troubleshooting"],[3465,"Troubleshooting"],[3478,"Troubleshooting"],[4006,"Troubleshooting"],[5276,"Troubleshooting"],[5567,"Troubleshooting"],[5048,"Troubleshooting for start-up errors."],[874,"Trust distribution"],[1214,"Trust distribution and updates"],[2064,"Trust model"],[1572,"Trusted issuers, validation and device permissions in the destination."],[2528,"Try **Sign / Verify** in KMS; check provider permissions and availability."],[2937,"Try hawkB